#!/bin/bash
# 715 (Sun 22 Nov 2026 17:49, Peter: "Check the goal signal to a real game ... no need to trade a game but see how accurate it
# is"). Small session: today's real matches, minute by minute - what the goal signal said against when the goals came.
# No trading, no paper. Read-only on everything else. Nothing deleted.
mkdir -p /root/pe-sports/goaltrade
cat > /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_scorecard.md <<'BRIEF'
You are a Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 22. Small job, light on memory (today's football only; other sessions are running). Work in /root/pe-sports/goaltrade (new files only). Read /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md (HARD RULES) and reports/goal_signal_audit.md. No trading, no paper, no trial entries: this is a scorecard only.
PETER'S ORDER: check the goal signal against real games, so he can SEE whether it predicts goals. No need to trade - just how accurate it is.
USE REAL, UNSEEN GAMES: every football match played yesterday and today (21 and 22 Nov 2026) for which we hold goal times and the signal's minute-by-minute output - both the old signal (goal_live_predictions / goal_alerts) and version 2 (goaltrade.signal_v2 and its scoring code; if version 2 was not running live for some of these matches, replay it on the recorded inputs using ONLY what was known at each minute, with each feed's measured delay, and say which matches were live and which replayed). Goal times from the score feed, cross-checked against the Betfair market's suspensions; say how many goals the two sources disagree on.
1. ONE MATCH LAID OUT SO HE CAN READ IT: pick the match with the most money matched today that has full data. A simple timeline, one line per 5 minutes from kick-off: minute, score, what version 2 said (chance of a goal in the next 10 minutes, as a % and as LOW / NORMAL / HIGH against the normal rate for that minute and score), what the old signal said, and what happened in the next 10 minutes (GOAL at minute X, or no goal). Mark each line RIGHT or WRONG for HIGH and LOW calls. Then the same for two more matches: the best-called and the worst-called of the day, so he sees both.
2. ALL THE MATCHES, as a scorecard: when version 2 said HIGH, how often a goal came in the next 10 minutes, against how often when it said NORMAL and LOW, and against the plain average for the same minutes and scores (count of calls and of matches for each). The same for the old signal. Then in one line each: of all goals scored, what share came while it was saying HIGH / NORMAL / LOW (and what share of the playing time each took, so the comparison is fair); how many minutes' warning a HIGH gave before the goal on average; how many HIGH calls passed with no goal. Then by half, by score state (level / one-goal game / two or more), and for the last 15 minutes.
3. THE HONEST SENTENCE at the top of the file: 'On N matches over two days, when the signal said HIGH a goal followed within 10 minutes X% of the time, against Y% normally; when it said LOW, Z%.' and whether two days is enough to trust that (give the range it could plausibly be).
4. WHAT WOULD MAKE IT SHARPER, from these games: look at the goals it missed (LOW or NORMAL just before a goal) and the HIGH calls with no goal - what did the match facts and the prices look like in those minutes; is there anything they have in common that it is not using (a red card, a substitution burst, a team chasing the game late, the over price refusing to drift, set pieces)? Three plain findings at most, each with its count. Do not rebuild the signal here; list what to try.
WRITE /root/pe-queue/reports/goal_signal_scorecard.md, plain English, one screen plus the three timelines. Leave a small plain loop (in /root/pe-mechanic/watch.list) that rewrites the scorecard section for the day's matches at 23:30 UK each night, appending one line per day to a running table (date, matches, HIGH hit rate, normal rate, LOW rate), so accuracy builds up day by day without anyone asking. Commit and push: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/goal_signal_scorecard.md && git commit -qm "goal signal against real games" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > /root/pe-builder/run_scorecard.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/goaltrade && timeout -k 60 9000 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_scorecard.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 250 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_scorecard.log 2>&1
echo "scorecard session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
SH
chmod +x /root/pe-builder/run_scorecard.sh
nohup setsid /root/pe-builder/run_scorecard.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & sleep 15; echo "scorecard session started: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_scorecard.sh')"
